# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO AT COUNTERPART: members Forge and NeoForge widen by PATCHING the vanilla jar
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both loaders ship a patched Minecraft in which a number of vanilla members are already public,
# so the AT never had to name them and there is no list to convert from. Fabric builds against
# the unpatched jar, so each one surfaces as a compile error on the node that first needs it.
#
# This section grows one entry at a time, as nodes are compiled. Do not populate it
# speculatively: an entry whose descriptor is wrong is a build failure, not the silent no-op the
# same mistake would be in the AT.

# The sprite-set half of particle registration. Vanilla keeps the callback interface private to
# ParticleEngine and only ever names it from registerSpriteSet's own parameter list; both other
# loaders widen it so a mod can hold one, and the Fabric stand-in for that registration phase
# takes the same parameter type so the six call sites read identically on every loader.
# 1.21.9 emptied ParticleEngine of every nested type — registration moved out to the new
# ParticleResources beside it, keeping the interface's shape byte for byte (ParticleProvider<T>
# create(SpriteSet)) and its package-private access. So this is one entry per side of the gate, and
# the Java side is a single !fab-mc2119-particle-registration rename rather than a source arm.
#? <1.21.9
accessible	class	net/minecraft/client/particle/ParticleEngine$SpriteParticleRegistration
#? >=1.21.9
accessible	class	net/minecraft/client/particle/ParticleResources$SpriteParticleRegistration

